1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,201
Presenter:

2
00:00:00,201 --> 00:00:04,131
The children’s book festival is coming up again soon and here to tell us

3
00:00:04,131 --> 00:00:07,908
all about it is the festival’s organiser, Jenny Morgan.

4
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:10,950
So tell us what we can expect this year, Jenny.

5
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:11,000
Jenny:

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,368
Well, as usual we’ve got five days of action-packed exciting events for children, with

7
00:00:18,368 --> 00:00:22,540
writers coming from all over the country getting involved.

8
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:26,170
Just to give you an idea of what’s on offer in the workshops

9
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:30,980
First of all, there’s a very special event called Superheroes.

10
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:37,447
This is a chance for deaf children to share their reading experiences with author

11
00:00:37,447 --> 00:00:38,370
Madeleine Gordon,

12
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:40,680
who is herself hearing impaired.

13
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:47,380
‘Just do it’ is a practical workshop led by the well-known illustrator Mark Keane.

14
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:54,069
He’ll take participants on a magical journey to faraway lands with an opportunity for

15
00:00:54,069 --> 00:00:57,190
aspiring actors to do some role play.

16
00:00:57,190 --> 00:01:03,572
‘Count on me’ is an inspiring and entertaining look at the issues of friendship

17
00:01:03,572 --> 00:01:04,940
for 13–14-year-olds.

18
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:09,649
It looks at some of the friendships described in popular books

19
00:01:09,649 --> 00:01:13,930
and asks participants to compare these with their own experiences.

20
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:19,530
‘Speak up’ is part of a series of workshops on the subject of mental

21
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:19,930
health.

22
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:27,735
This is a creative writing workshop encouraging children to describe situations where young people

23
00:01:27,735 --> 00:01:28,850
experience loneliness.

24
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:34,942
A recent survey revealed that children can be lonely even when they’re at home

25
00:01:34,942 --> 00:01:36,160
with their families.

26
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:41,538
‘Jump for joy’, as many of you will know, is the heart-warming, best-selling story

27
00:01:41,538 --> 00:01:46,916
by Nina Karan about a young girl’s trip to visit her relatives in

28
00:01:46,916 --> 00:01:47,300
India.

29
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:51,743
It recently received the gold medal at the Waterford Awards.

30
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:57,520
Nina will get children to celebrate the word ‘joy’ by writing a poem.

31
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:03,830
‘Sticks and stones’ is the beautifully illustrated picture book for young readers about a

32
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:10,139
community who organise an African-Caribbean festival to help local children learn about their Jamaican

33
00:02:10,139 --> 00:02:10,590
roots.

34
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:16,297
This will be a musical event where children will have the chance to play

35
00:02:16,297 --> 00:02:17,113
steel drums.

36
00:02:17,113 --> 00:02:19,966
This is bound to be very popular,

37
00:02:19,966 --> 00:02:22,820
so please book as soon as possible.

38
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:23,180
Presenter:

39
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,977
That all sounds really interesting.

40
00:02:24,977 --> 00:02:30,370
I’m just wondering if you have a favourite book you could recommend for our

41
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:30,730
readers?

42
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:31,149
Jenny:

43
00:02:31,149 --> 00:02:33,246
It’s hard to choose,

44
00:02:33,246 --> 00:02:36,600
but Alive and Kicking is definitely worth mentioning.

45
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:41,092
You won’t have heard of the writer as it’s her first book – which

46
00:02:41,092 --> 00:02:41,990
is really impressive.

47
00:02:41,990 --> 00:02:47,207
It’s basically the teenage diary of a boy from Somalia who comes to live

48
00:02:47,207 --> 00:02:48,250
in the UK.

49
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:54,961
It deals with the serious issue of immigration and all the challenges the boy has to face at school

50
00:02:54,961 --> 00:02:57,080
and with the language barrier, etc.

51
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:00,300
Usually, books like this are quite sad,

52
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:04,440
but this one actually made me cry with laughter.

53
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:10,755
On each page, there are simple but hilarious black and white stick drawings of the boy with his friends

54
00:03:10,755 --> 00:03:11,420
and teachers.

55
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:16,370
At the end of each diary entry, there are new English words the boy

56
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:17,431
learns each day,

57
00:03:17,431 --> 00:03:20,260
which may help develop some children’s vocabulary.

58
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:20,605
Presenter:

59
00:03:20,605 --> 00:03:23,022
I think my kids would enjoy that.

60
00:03:23,022 --> 00:03:27,855
What about any advice for parents on how to encourage their children to read

61
00:03:27,855 --> 00:03:28,200
more?

62
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:28,540
Jenny:

63
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:31,940
Well, this is something I get asked about a lot.

64
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:35,882
There are so many distractions for kids these days that it can be hard

65
00:03:35,882 --> 00:03:37,290
to find time for reading.

66
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:41,452
One thing I’d say is to make time to sit down with your child

67
00:03:41,452 --> 00:03:42,840
and share books with them.

68
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:47,221
A lot of parents give up reading aloud to their children as soon as

69
00:03:47,221 --> 00:03:48,785
they learn to read independently,

70
00:03:48,785 --> 00:03:50,350
but this is a mistake.

71
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:55,318
It’s good to read more advanced books to them as it helps to develop

72
00:03:55,318 --> 00:03:55,980
their vocabulary.

73
00:03:55,980 --> 00:04:00,170
If you don’t have time for this, then let them listen to audio

74
00:04:00,170 --> 00:04:00,469
books.

75
00:04:00,469 --> 00:04:04,360
Often, they’ll want to read books they’ve listened to for themselves.

76
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:08,755
I think it’s a good idea to make a mental note of the type

77
00:04:08,755 --> 00:04:12,563
of books your child is reading – often they just read the same genre

78
00:04:12,563 --> 00:04:13,442
all the time,

79
00:04:13,442 --> 00:04:15,200
which can get a bit boring.

80
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:18,235
You can introduce new authors and genres to them.

81
00:04:18,235 --> 00:04:21,270
Librarians should be able to help you with this.

82
00:04:21,270 --> 00:04:21,533
Presenter:

83
00:04:21,533 --> 00:04:23,640
Well Jenny, I think that’s really useful.

84
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:23,690
.

85
00:04:23,690 --> 00:04:23,740
.

86
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:23,790
.
